# The annotations of the Nether Earth ZS Edition '99 game block ($6780-$D7D2 as unpacked): the single source
# of truth for src/game99/*.asm (tools/game99_mkgame.py -> kit/mkgame.py). Format: kit/mkgame.py, class
# Annotations. Seeded from Nether Earth's game.ann where the bytes are the same; every entry is checked for
# this game.


@module start ; the start-up, the new game set-up and the play loop
@about The game's top level. Start ($A600) is entered from stage 3's last JP (`$631B` on 128 K, `$63C4` on the tape path), from the end of a game ($AEFD), from key I in play ($A712) and from LOAD MAP (`$C1FD` jumps here after a map is read or refused). Its first instruction calls the start-up menu through $C100, which stage 3 has filled with a piece of the patches block (src/patches99: menu128 or menu48); the menu returns to StartGame ($A603) when START is chosen. StartGame builds the IM 2 table, seeds the random generator and falls into NewGameSetup, which clears the game state and builds the map; EnterPlay draws the play screen and switches to PlayInterrupt; PlayLoop runs one game turn a pass, forever.
@about The routines in calling order: Start ($A600) -> the start-up menu ($C100) -> StartGame ($A603) -> NewGameSetup ($A62D) -> EnterPlay ($A68E) -> PlayLoop ($A69A). Each falls into the next; nothing here returns. There is no saved game: the loaded-game skip at $A62D and the save key in PlayLoop ($A715) are gone, NOPs in their place.
@about The IM 2 trick: 257 bytes of $FD at $FE00-$FF00 with I = $FE make the vector $FDFD whatever the data bus holds; a JP sits at $FDFD and its operand Handler ($FDFE) is the only thing the game changes to switch the interrupt's work (StartInterrupt while the world stands still, PlayInterrupt in play).
@about PlayLoop is the game's state machine in one loop: WaitKey, MoveShip, WorldTurn, DrawView, GameTurn; then what lies under the ship decides whether a menu opens: our robot with the ship on its top opens RobotMenu (robotmenu.asm), the pad of our warbase at altitude 15 with a free door opens ConstructionMenu (construction.asm), and PlayLoop writes the new robot's start state after it. Last the abort key I.
@about The module owns no data; NewGameSetup zeroes the robot records ($DA00), the variables ($FD04-$FDFC), Buildings and Shots. The bug of the full robot table (the new-robot writes land on enemy slot 24) is kept. MenuInterrupt ($C4FD), the handler of the beeper title music, is listed here; nothing installs it any more.
$A600 c Start ; the entry: the start-up menu, then StartGame
> CALL $C100: the start-up menu, which stage 3 has placed there (the patches block's menu128 or menu48 piece: on 128 K it copies the menu from page 1 to $D800 in page 0 and runs it there). The piece keeps SP for the menu, so the menu's START returns through this CALL to StartGame at $A603, after the level's routine (`$DA8E`) has written the level's code and operands into this block.
> The block's own bytes of the menu at $C100 are zeros (mainmenu.asm); without stage 3 the CALL would run them as NOPs into whatever follows.
$A600 ; the start-up menu (stage 3's piece at $C100); START returns here
$A603 c StartGame ; After START: the IM 2 table, Handler = StartInterrupt, the random seed, then a new game
> Reached when the start-up menu returns (START). DI, SP = 0; the IM 2 table $FE00-$FF00 (257 x $FD), JP at $FDFD, Handler = StartInterrupt, I = $FE, IM 2, EI; the random seed $FD00-$FD03 = $39,$30,$39,$30. Falls into NewGameSetup: every game is a new one.
$A603 ; no interrupts while the vector table is built
$A604 ; SP = 0: the stack grows down from $FFFF
$A607 ; the IM 2 table: 257 bytes of $FD at $FE00-$FF00
$A60A ; B = 0: 256 bytes, C = $FD the byte stored
$A60D FillVectorTable ; one vector byte
$A60E ; the next
$A60F ; 256 times
$A611 ; the 257th byte, $FF00: the vector $FEFF (bus byte $FF) reads its high byte here
$A612 ; the JP opcode
$A614 ; at $FDFD: every vector lands on Handler
$A617 ; StartInterrupt (EI; RET) until EnterPlay
$A61A ; Handler = StartInterrupt
$A61D ; I = $FE: the vector table's page
$A61F ; I points at $FE00
$A621 ; IM 2: the interrupt jumps through ($FE00 + the bus byte) = $FDFD
$A623 ; interrupts on from here
$A624 ; the random seed $39,$30 (12345)
$A627 ; $FD00-$FD01
$A62A ; $FD02-$FD03: the same seed at every start, so each game begins the same
$C4FD c MenuInterrupt ; the interrupt handler of the menu
> The drum, the bass and lead note readers, the title colours; a number key ends MenuMusic. It returns to the tone loop of MenuMusic, or, with SP from SavedSP, to the menu.
$C4FD ; the tone loop's A and flags
$C4FF ; DrumStep: one drum record, with the tone loop stopped
$C504 ; BassNextEvent: the next note or commands of the bass
$C508 ; LeadNextEvent: the next note or commands of the lead
$C510 ; IN from $E7FE: the half rows 1-5 and 6-0
$C512 ; a pressed key reads 0: invert
$C513 ; the five key bits
$C515 ; a number key is down
$C518 ; the tone loop's A and flags back
$C519 ; interrupts on for the next frame
$C51A ; into the tone loop where it was
$C520 ; Handler back to StartInterrupt (EI; RET)
$C523 ; nothing runs in the menu's HALTs now
$C526 ; interrupts on
$C527 ; returns from MenuMusic to the menu ($C1A1)
@module interrupts ; the frame interrupts of play, the radar, the shot sounds
@about The IM 2 handlers: the vector $FDFD jumps to Handler ($FDFE), which Start, EnterPlay and the menus switch. PlayInterrupt runs once a frame in play: TurnFrames + 1 (the play loop's 10-frame turn), one of the two radar pictures (ShowRadar, by RadarPhase), and one step of the running sound effect (SoundEffect, by SoundFx). StartInterrupt is EI; RET, the handler while the world must stand still (the start-up, the construction menu, saving, the game's end). The menu's handler MenuInterrupt is in the start module.
@about The radar: two pictures of 16 lines x 16 bytes, RadarOwn $D800 (the terrain + the player's robots, shown in $4D, cyan on blue) and RadarEnemy $D900 (the terrain + the enemy's, $4E, yellow on blue), one pixel a map cell for the 128 columns from RadarColumn. Shown in turns every frame at rows 22-23, columns 6-21: the terrain stays, each side's robots blink at 25 Hz in their colour. BuildRadar makes both from the map; ToggleRadarDot (with RadarDotAddress) XORs one robot's 2 x 2 dot in or out.
@about The sound: SoundFx is set by the shot code (1 a shot fired, $FC a miss, $FA a hit, $C8 a robot destroyed) and counted on by the interrupt. 2-$7F: a burst of pulses from the ROM's bytes, fewer and further apart each frame, a falling sweep until $80; $C8-$FF: 30 random speaker levels a frame until it wraps to 0. At most 3.2 ms a frame.
@about The interrupt runs with interrupts off (the CPU's acceptance) and turns them on only at its last EI. The radar costs 8134 T a frame, 11 % of all the time in play.
$D566 c PlayInterrupt ; the interrupt handler of play, one a frame
> Installed by EnterPlay ($A694) and when the construction menu ends ($CBA2). Keeps AF, BC, DE, HL (not IX, IY, the alternate set: the routines it calls do not use them). TurnFrames + 1 (TurnPace $AE1D waits for 10, then zeroes it; RadarPhase toggled, ShowRadar with RadarOwn / $4D on phase 0, RadarEnemy / $4E on phase 1; SoundFx not 0: counted on (a tone ends at $80, a noise when it wraps to 0) and SoundEffect plays this frame's step. Ends in StartInterrupt's EI; RET.
$D566 ; keep the interrupted code's registers
$D567 ; (BC)
$D568 ; (DE)
$D569 ; (HL)
$D56D ; one more frame of this turn
$D571 ; its attribute: bright cyan on blue
$D576 ; toggle
$D578 ; kept for the next frame
$D57B ; phase 0: the player's picture
$D580 ; bright yellow on blue
$D588 ; (0: silent)
$D589 ; silent: on to the exit
$D58C ; the tone's end?
$D58E ; no: keep the count
$D590 ; the tone is over: silent
$D591 InterruptSoundStore
$D595 ; this frame's burst (the flags of the OR above choose its kind)
$D598 InterruptExit
$D599 ; (DE)
$D59A ; (BC)
$D59B ; (AF; on into StartInterrupt
$D59C c StartInterrupt ; the interrupt handler while the start-up runs
> Also the end of PlayInterrupt. Installed alone by Start (FillVectorTable $A614), GameStop ($AF0A), SaveGame ($C28D), the end of MenuMusic ($C520) and OpenConstructionMenu ($C85E): no frame counting, no radar, so the world stands while it is in place.
$D59C ; interrupts on again (the CPU turned them off on acceptance)
$D59D ; back to the interrupted code
@module ship ; the player's ship: its move, altitude, view follow, radar window and map mark
@about The ship is the player's only body: a flying cursor that moves one cell a game turn over the 512 x 16 map, climbs on fire and sinks onto robots and warbase pads, where the robot menu and the construction menu open.
@about MoveShip runs once a game turn from PlayLoop (and from UpdateRobot while a robot is under direct control). In calling order: LiftShipFromMap takes the ship's mark off the map; the X move (MoveShipX), the view follow (FollowView), the Y move (MoveShipY) and the altitude (AutoClimb, ClimbByFire, Sink) follow, each step tested by CheckShipMove; then ScrollRadar moves the radar window, RadarBlink counts and PutShipOnMap puts the mark back, falling into RefreshRadar and BlinkShipDot. MenuRadarTick is the blink step the robot menus call each pass.
@about CheckShipMove gives the highest top under the ship's 2 x 2 footprint: CellTop (the terrain through TerrainTop, then ObjectTop) for the four own cells, ObjectTop alone for the five cells left of and below them, whose anchored objects reach into the footprint. KeepHigher is the shared max. The ship may not stand lower than that top.
@about Data it owns: ShipX ($FD0E, kept in $0E-$1F5), ShipY ($FD0D, rows 1-15), Altitude ($FD10, 0-$30), ClimbTurns ($FD30), ViewX / MapView (the view's left column and its map address), RadarLeft / RadarColumn ($FD1B / $FD1C), RadarBlink ($FD1E), RadarDirty ($FD52), and BuildingTops (the heights of the 9 building types). The ship's map mark is bit 7 of its cell.
@about Tricks: the ship has no record. Its map mark (bit 7) is lifted before the move and set on the new cell after it; the robots' probes read it. The ship's radar dot is XORed on odd RadarBlink ticks on both sides of the move: it blinks every other turn.
$AF11 c MoveShip ; the ship's move from Input: X by right / left, Y by down / up, each refused by CheckShipMove
> Also the auto-climb (ClimbTurns), the altitude (fire +2 up to $30, else -1 down to what stands under the ship), the view follow (ViewX moves when the ship is at view column 13 or 22), the radar window (ScrollRadar) and the ship's mark: map bit 7 cleared on the old cell ($B01D) and set on the new one ($B024), the radar blip XORed. Called by PlayLoop, and by UpdateRobot ($B4A8) while a robot is under direct control.
