$AD2C ; D = the item, E = the items + 1
$AD2D ; check the robot, the next key
$AD30 ; attribute $4F
$AD32 ; paint the chosen item
$AD37 ; beep
$AD3B ; A = the item chosen
$AD57 c WaitRelease ; wait until no control is held
> Calls WaitKey until Input is 0: every control up. Used by every menu before it reads a new press, so the fire that chose an item does not choose again. Keeps BC and HL; A = 0 on return. Uses WaitKey, so key 1 still pauses.
$AD57 ; kept
$AD58 ; kept
$AD5C ; anything held?
$AD5D ; still held: read again
$AD5F ; HL back
$AD60 ; BC back
$AD61 ; all up
@module construction ; the construction menu, robot placement on the map, the beeper tone
@about The screen the player gets by landing the ship on the pad of our warbase, the rules. PlayLoop calls ConstructionMenu; it takes a free slot among our 24 robot records (IY) as the preview, builds the screen and runs its own key loop until START ROBOT or EXIT MENU; then the play screen is redrawn and PlayLoop gives the new robot its start state.
@about In calling order: ConstructionMenu (find the slot, the part pictures, the text) -> ConstructionMenuSetup -> ConstructionMenuLoop (fire on a part: PartToggle, RefundPart; a direction: MoveMenuCursor) -> MenuPause after every press -> StartRobot (PlaceRobot) or LeaveConstructionMenu. Helpers: PaintMenuCursor and FillAttributes, PrintMenuResources (PrintMenuResource, PrintMenuTotal), ShowRobotDesign (the part colours and the preview robot drawn by DrawRobot through the view buffer).
@about The data: MenuCursor = (L the column: 0 EXIT MENU, 1 START ROBOT, 2 the parts; H the part row 0 bipod... 7 electronics), MenuParts = the chosen parts, one bit a row (the new robot's +$07), MenuResources = the copy of Resources the menu spends, written back only by START ROBOT. PartCosts holds the eight costs and, 8 bytes on, the resource each part pays from first; the general resource pays the rest.
@about The world stands still while the menu is open: the menu loop runs no game turns, and Handler is StartInterrupt (no frame count, no radar drawing over the menu screen). MenuPause is 10 HALTs after every press; a press inside it is lost. PrintMenuResource patches the row byte of its own inline position string ($CC11) before each print.
@about The tail of the module is shared with the rest of the game: PlaceRobot puts any robot on the map (the robot step, the enemy spawn, START ROBOT), ShipCell / MapCell give the map cell of a column and row ($DD00 + $200 x row + column), Beep is the menus' beeper tone.
$C849 c ConstructionMenu ; landing on the warbase pad at altitude 15: choose the parts (MenuCursor, MenuParts), START ROBOT or EXIT MENU
> Called by PlayLoop when the ship rests on the pad of our warbase (Altitude 15) and the door below is free. First looks for a free record among our slots 0-23 ($DA00 + 16 x n, +$01 = 0; with none it returns at once (IY = $DB80, enemy slot 24: the bug of PlayLoop's writes.
> Otherwise IY = the free slot: the preview robot, and on START ROBOT the new one. Handler = StartInterrupt, so neither the frame count nor the radar runs; the menu loop runs no game turns, so the world stands still while the menu is open. ClearScreen, then the eight part pictures in column 16 from the bottom (bipod) to the top (electronics), each the second facing of the part's sprite cut to 24 lines and drawn upwards by DrawMaskedUp; the preview's frame attributes ($46, 4 x 9 cells from row 15, column 0; the texts (title, part names, EXIT / START, costs, the resource labels). Falls into ConstructionMenuSetup.
> Returns (after LeaveConstructionMenu) with IY = the slot; PlayLoop then writes the start state. On EXIT MENU the slot stays free (+$01 = 0, PlaceRobot not called), so those writes do no harm.
