@module level ; The built-in level: the scenery and base lists, the shape table and the 11 shapes BuildMap turns into the map
@about The level BuildMap ($BC6F) expands into Map at every new game (the map itself is zero in the image): the four lists at $BDA9-$BF9B, then ShapeTable and the shapes. The lists' room is $1F3 bytes, the size of a NELEV file before its tail: LOAD MAP copies a file's first $1F3 bytes here and its tail into BuildMap's operands, so a loaded map replaces this one until the program is loaded again.
@about The built-in level is a placeholder: the player's warbase at (18,8) between two edge fences, the enemy's at (497,8) between two more, no factory, and between them, in tall blocks and walls, the Russian word ЗАГРУЗИСЬ ("load yourself"): the radar shows it at the first game, telling the player to load a map with LOAD MAP. Resource src/res/maps/builtin.txt (tools/zs_level.py, the NELEV text without the tail; preview src/res/maps/png/builtin.png). It has two capture points (the operands $ADB4, $BB1F) and both warbases: a capture is handed to record 26 or 27 (GameTurnEnd), so the enemy's warbase can only be destroyed.
@about Order of use: PlaceScenery ($BCD6) walks SceneryWest (D = 0, columns 0-255) and SceneryEast (D = 1, 256-511): a 2 x 2 tile block (PutBlock) or a shape of ShapeTable (DrawShape $BD61); then PlaceBases ($BCF9) walks BaseListWest / BaseListEast, draws shape 0 (warbase) or 1 (factory) at each and fills the capture points (Warbases, Factories) and the buildings. MarkBaseDestroyed ($BC1C) walks a base's shape again with debris tiles. The shapes (src/res/game99/map/shapes.txt) are the same bytes as Nether Earth's.
@about Formats: scenery record (tile or $80 + shape, x low byte, y), 0 ends; base record (type 0 warbase / 1-6 factory, x low byte, y), $FF ends; ShapeTable 11 words; a shape is 3-byte steps (tile, dx, dy): tile 0 only moves, a step with move (0, 0) ends the shape after its block.
$BDA9 b SceneryWest 3 ; The scenery of columns 0-255: 55 records (tile or shape, x, y) and the 0 that ends them
@res maps/builtin.bin
> Read by BuildMap through PlaceScenery with D = 0. Bit 7 clear: a 2 x 2 block of that tile; bit 7 set: shape (byte & $7F) of ShapeTable. Here: four edge fences (shape 6) at columns 12 and 24, around the player's warbase, and the letters of ЗАГРУЗИСЬ at columns 30-101, rows 3-12: tile $11 (low block) for the З, tile $12 (tall block) and tall walls (shape 4) for the rest.
$BE4F b SceneryEast 3 ; The scenery of columns 256-511: 4 records and the 0 that ends them
@res maps/builtin.bin
> Read by BuildMap through PlaceScenery with D = 1 (x + 256): four edge fences (shape 6) at columns 491 and 503, rows 1 and 9, around the enemy's warbase.
$BE5C b BaseListWest 3 ; The bases of columns 0-255: the player's warbase at (18,8), $FF ends
@res maps/builtin.bin
> Type 0 a warbase, 1-6 a factory making resource 1-6 (electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis). The (x, y) is the base's door. The first warbase placed is record 0, the player's.
$BE60 b BaseListEast 3 ; The bases of columns 256-511: the enemy's warbase at (497,8), $FF ends
@res maps/builtin.bin
> Record 1, given to the enemy by BuildMap's loop.
$BE64 s LevelSpare ; The rest of the lists' room: zero up to $BF9B
@res maps/builtin.bin
> 312 zero bytes. A NELEV file's lists may fill the room up to $BF9B: LOAD MAP copies $1F3 bytes from $BDA9 whatever the lists use.
$BF9C w ShapeTable 11 ; The shapes' addresses, index 0-10 (DrawShape)
@res map/shape-table.bin
> 0 the warbase, 1 the factory (both drawn by PlaceBases), 2-10 the scenery shapes (a scenery record $82-$8A).
$BFB2 b ShapeWarbase 3 ; Shape 0: the warbase, 15 blocks of tiles $0F (height 7) and $10 (height 15) around the door
@res map/shapes/warbase.bin
> Steps (tile, columns, rows): a first move of (-4,-4), then blocks reaching 4 columns either side and up to 8 rows above the door. The pad, the block above the door at (x, y-4), is a tile $10 block: altitude 15 over it is the landing height of ConstructionMenu.
$BFE2 b ShapeFactory 3 ; Shape 1: the factory, tiles $0F at (x-2, y) and (x+2, y), $10 on the roof row y-2 at x-2, x, x+2
@res map/shapes/factory.bin
> The door (x, y) stays free between the two low blocks.
$BFF4 b ShapeLowTiles 3 ; Shape 2: 4 x 3 blocks of tiles $02-$05 (height 2)
@res map/shapes/low-tiles.bin
> Steps (tile, dx, dy): 12 blocks in 3 rows of 4, each block 2 cells right of the last, a row 2 cells down (-6, 2; the tiles cycle through the four stone pictures. Drawn by DrawShape for scenery records $82 (5 placed). Ground height 2: the ship and robots pass over it.
$C018 b ShapeMidTiles 3 ; Shape 3: 4 x 3 blocks of tiles $08-$0B (height 6)
@res map/shapes/mid-tiles.bin
> The rock version of ShapeLowTiles: the same 3 x 4 layout with tiles $08-$0B (height 6). Scenery $83, 10 placed.
$C03C b ShapeTallWallV 3 ; Shape 4: four blocks of tile $12 (height 15) in a column
@res map/shapes/tall-wall-v.bin
> Steps of (0, 2): a wall 2 cells thick, 8 rows long, tile $12 (the tall block). Scenery $84, 3 placed. A nuclear blast can turn its blocks to debris.
$C048 b ShapeTallWallH 3 ; Shape 5: four blocks of tile $12 in a row
@res map/shapes/tall-wall-h.bin
> Steps of (2, 0): the same wall along X, 8 columns long. Scenery $85, 8 placed.
$C054 b ShapeEdgeFence 3 ; Shape 6: four blocks of tile $15 (height 99, impassable) in a column: the fences at both ends of the map
@res map/shapes/edge-fence.bin
> Placed at (12,1), (12,9), (503,1), (503,9): the row of posts at the map's left and right edges.
$C060 b ShapeFlatLineV 3 ; Shape 7: tiles $0C, $0D, $0D, $0E (height 0) in a column
@res map/shapes/flat-line-v.bin
> Steps of (0, 2): a trench along Y: start $0C, middle $0D twice, end $0E (height 0, drawn sunken). Scenery $87, 7 placed.
$C06C b ShapeFlatLineH 3 ; Shape 8: tiles $0C, $0D, $0D, $0E in a row
