@module music ; The menu tune: a two-voice beeper loop, the drum and the note readers in the interrupt, the title colours
@about NE's beeper tune of the main menu (MenuMusic $C4A7, MenuInterrupt $C4FD and the tracks from $C665) and the colour cycle of the title. The '99 program never plays the tune: its start-up menu is silent (no OUT to a sound port, no Beep call), and nothing calls MenuMusic. TitleColourCycle ($C820) is still called, by the start-up menu, for its title rows. The tune's tracks are src/res/game99/music/menu.txt (tools/game99_res.py, the menu tune text).
@about Calling order: MainMenuKeys ($C19E) calls MenuMusic, which saves SP in SavedSP, makes MenuInterrupt the Handler, sets the track positions and never returns by itself: it runs the tone loop (LeadStep / BassStep, each voice a square-ish pulse train counted in loop turns by D and E). Every frame MenuInterrupt plays the next drum record (DrumStep, which stops the tone loop for its length), reads the next bass and lead events when their frames run out (BassNextEvent, LeadNextEvent, commands through MusicDispatch and the two JP tables), recolours the title (TitleColourCycle) and, when a number key is down, loads SP from SavedSP and returns from MenuMusic itself (MenuMusicExit).
@about Registers own the state: the main set's D / E are the lead / bass period counters, HL the drum position, C the drum's frames left; the alternate set's HL' the bass position, DE' the lead position, C' / B' the bass / lead frames left. The interrupt saves only AF. The periods, the pulse widths and the on / off values are operands of the tone loop's own instructions, written by the readers (self-modifying code: $C4D0, $C4D2, $C4D6, $C4ED, $C4EF, $C4F3).
@about Tracks: a note is (period >= 9, frames; a byte below 9 is a command: 1 aa goto, 2 aa call a pattern (one level, the return in BassReturnPos / LeadReturnPos), 3 aa start a drum track (bass only), 4 on, 5 off, 6 return; 0, 7, 8 unused. Drum records are (frames, kind[, p]): 0 a hiss, 2 p a falling sweep, others a rasp; 1 aa goto. The tune is 5120 frames (102.4 s) and loops.
@about Quirks: MenuMusic does not reset the on / off operands, so a tune restarted (after keys 1-3) while a voice was off stays silent until that voice's next command 4 (docs 9.4). Command 3 in the lead table would use the bass position. The exit leaves the alternate set swapped in; the menu keeps nothing in registers.
$C4A7 c MenuMusic ; play the menu tune until a number key is pressed
> Called once by the main menu ($C19E) after all keys are up. Saves SP in $FD08, installs MenuInterrupt as Handler and sets the music state: the bass voice reads the track $C678 through HL' (frames left C'), the lead voice the track $C702 through DE' (frames left B'), the drum the track $C665 through HL (frames left C; C' = B' = C = 1, so the first interrupt reads the first event of each. Then it runs the tone loop for ever: the main set's D and E count each voice's period down.
> The tone loop never returns: MenuInterrupt ends it by loading SP from $FD08 when a number key is down, so the RET of the interrupt returns from MenuMusic to the menu ($C1A1), which then reads the key. The tune is 5120 frames (102.
$C4AC ; Handler: the IM 2 vector at $FDFD jumps to MenuInterrupt now
$C4AF ; Handler = MenuInterrupt
$C4BB ; HL, DE, BC become HL', DE', BC'
$C4BF ; the interrupt may run (the HALT below waits for it)
$C4C0 ; C = 1: the drum's first record at the first interrupt
$C4A7 ; no interrupt while the state is set
$C4A8 ; the stack for the exit from MenuInterrupt
$C4B2 ; HL' = the bass track
$C4B5 ; DE' = the lead track
$C4B8 ; B' = C' = 1: read both voices at the first interrupt
$C4BC ; HL = the drum track, C = 1: read it at the first interrupt
$C4C2 ; start on an interrupt
$C4C3 c BassIdlePad ; tone loop: the time of a bass step without an edge
> The tone loop runs LeadStep and BassStep in turn, about 122 T a turn. A step whose counter has not run out takes this pad (PUSH AF, read Input and write it back unchanged, POP AF: 61 T with the DEC / JP) so both paths take about the same time, the pitch stays steady whatever the other voice does.
$C4C4 ; 13 T: a read of Input...
$C4C7 ; ... and the same value back, 13 T
$C4CA ; A and the flags back: the pad leaves no trace
$C4C3 ; a pad: Input is written back unchanged, only the time counts
$C4CB c LeadStep ; tone loop: count the lead period down, at 0 a pulse
> D counts the lead voice's period in loop turns. At 0 it reloads D with the note's period (the operand $C4D0), sets the beeper (EAR and MIC, $18, or 0 when the voice is off: the operand $C4D2) for a pulse of 14 * width T (the operand $C4D6 = period / 4, at most 63), then clears it. The voice's pitch is about 3.5 MHz / (122 + 3.5) / period: period $7C about 225 Hz, $29 about 680 Hz.
$C4CC ; not run out: the pad, then the bass
$C4D8 ; until the width is used up
$C4DD ; on to the bass voice
$C4E1 ; 13 T: Input read...
$C4CB ; the lead counter
$C4CF ; reload: the note's period, written by LeadNextEvent ($C540); the image holds $29, the last note played before the block was saved
$C4D1 ; $18 voice on, 0 off: written by LeadOn / LeadOff
$C4D3 ; the pulse's leading edge
$C4D5 ; the pulse width: period / 4, written by $C547 ($0A in the image)
$C4D7 LeadPulse ; hold the pulse
$C4DB ; A = 0: the trailing edge
$C4E0 LeadIdlePad ; no edge: the same pad as BassIdlePad
$C4E4 c LeadIdlePadTail ; the rest of the lead pad
> Write Input back and POP AF, then fall into BassStep.
$C4E4 ; ... and written back unchanged
$C4E7 ; A and the flags back
$C4E8 c BassStep ; tone loop: count the bass period down, at 0 a pulse
> The bass voice's twin of LeadStep: E is its counter, the operands $C4ED (period), $C4EF (on / off) and $C4F3 (pulse width) are written by BassNextEvent, BassOn and BassOff.
$C4E9 ; not run out: the pad, then the lead
$C4F0 ; the pulse's leading edge
$C4F5 ; until the width is used up
$C4F8 ; A = 0: the trailing edge
$C4E8 ; the bass counter
$C4EC ; reload: the note's period, written by BassNextEvent ($C52D); $7C in the image
$C4EE ; $18 voice on, 0 off: written by BassOn / BassOff (0 in the image: the bass was off when the block was saved)
$C4F2 ; the pulse width: period / 4, written by $C534 ($1F in the image)
$C4F4 BassPulse ; hold the pulse
$C4FA ; next turn: the lead voice

$C4FE ; the drum's frames run out: play the next drum record (inside the interrupt, the tone loop waits)
$C502 ; the voices' registers
$C503 ; the bass note's frames run out: the next bass event
$C507 ; the lead note's frames run out: the next lead event
$C50B ; the title's colours
$C50E ; half-rows 1-5 and 6-0: a number key, any menu choice
$C517 ; none: back to the tone loop
$C51B MenuMusicExit ; a number key: drop the tone loop, return from MenuMusic to the menu
$C51C ; SP as MenuMusic left it: the next RET returns to $C1A1

$C528 c BassNextEvent ; read the bass voice's next event: a note, or a command
> HL' = the bass track position. A byte of 9 or more is a note: the byte is the period (written into $C4ED, and period / 4 into the pulse width $C4F3) and the next byte its length in frames (C'). A byte below 9 is a command (BassCommand). Called by MenuInterrupt when C' runs out, and jumped to again by every command handler, so commands take no time.
