@module view ; the isometric view: the buffer, the painter's walk over the map, the masked sprites
@about The play area, redrawn from nothing every game turn into the 160 x 160 pixel buffer ViewBuffer ($5B00-$677F, 20 bytes x 160 lines) and copied to the screen at character (1,1). Nothing scrolls and nothing is erased: moving the view is only ViewX + 1. The redraw takes about 8.3 frames and is why a game turn is 10 frames.
@about Calling order: DrawView = DrawViewBackground (ClearViewBuffer, FillViewCorner, DrawEdgeStrips: the corners outside the map) + DrawMapObjects (the ship's shadow, then the 32 x 16 visible cells along diagonals back to front) + CopyViewToScreen. Each cell goes to DrawCell: its block (sprite = the type), then DrawCellObject (a robot by FindRobot, a building by FindBuilding, else a shot) and the ship. Every picture ends in DrawSprite.
@about The depth order is the walk itself, there is no sort. An object a later neighbouring block would paint over is deferred: DrawMover (robots, shots) and DrawCell (the ship) look for the latest-drawn neighbour (FindLatestBlock, CheckObjectNeighbour, CheckBlockNeighbour, KeepLaterCell), mark that cell (bit 6, or bit 7 for the ship) and draw when the walk reaches it, at the real position.
@about DrawSprite is the projection: half-byte column L = 2E + D - 24, bottom line H = 8D + 100 - 4E - height. Sprites (SpriteTable $D6E8, two pre-shifted pictures each) are (mask, pixels) pairs from the bottom line up, drawn with AND / OR and clipped on all four sides. Robots are stacks of part sprites (DrawRobot, DrawRobotPart: PartSpriteTable $D6C8, PartHeights $D7B4; buildings take BuildingSprites / BuildingHeights by type.
@about Self-modified operands: the height of the next sprite at $CF3F (DrawSprite+$12, written by every caller), the left / right cut at $CFAF, the corner fill and skip counts at $D038 / $D041. ClearViewBuffer clears with PUSH (SP parked in SavedSP).
$CCBD c DrawView ; Draw the isometric view: background, objects, copy to the screen
> DrawViewBackground clears the buffer $5B00 and paints the corners outside the map, DrawMapObjects draws every block, building, robot, shot and the ship, CopyViewToScreen puts the 160x160 buffer on the screen. About 578000 T-states a call, 8.3 frames: the reason a turn is 10 frames.
> Called by PlayLoop, the menu loops that keep the game going (ListMenu's turn $AD1F, DirectControlLoop, DistanceLoop, CombatFireShot), the nuclear blast ($BA6D) and DrawPlayScreen. No HALT before the copy: the screen may tear for a frame.
$CCBD ; clear the buffer, paint the corners and the map's edge
$CCC0 ; the shadow, the blocks, buildings, robots, shots and the ship
$CCC3 ; the buffer to the screen; its RET ends DrawView
$CCC6 c DrawMapObjects ; Draw the 32 x 16 cells of the view back to front into the view buffer
> Clears the deferred ship position $FD11 and draws the ship's shadow (sprite 1, height 0) at the ship's view column (ShipX - ViewX) and row ShipY. Then walks the visible part of the map along diagonals: the diagonal x - y = 31 first down to x - y = -15, each from row 0 (x, y) to (x+15, y+15) or the right edge; cells left of the view (x < 0) and cells that are 0 are skipped, every other cell goes to DrawCell with HL = the cell, D = row, E = view column. The order is the painter's order of the projection (DrawSprite): a later cell is nearer the viewer.
