> Index = the Buildings type: 0 the player's warbase pad $2C (a crate), factories 1 electronics $28, 2 nuclear $25, 3 phasers $23, 4 missiles $20, 5 cannon $1D, 6 chassis $17 (each the robot part of its product, facing down: $16 + the parts table $D6C8), 7 and 8 the flags $2A and $2B. 7 the player's, 8 the enemy's.
> The bytes: $2C, $28, $25, $23, $20, $1D, $17, $2A, $2B. Read by DrawBuilding.
$CE56 ; by building type: 0 pad 44 (the warbase sign), factories 1 electronics 40, 2 nuclear 37, 3 phasers 35, 4 missiles 32, 5 cannon 29, 6 chassis 23, flags 7 ours 42, 8 the enemy's 43
$CE5F b BuildingHeights 9 ; The height each building sprite is drawn at
> Type 0 at $13, the six factory signs at $0F (on the factory's roof blocks), the flags at $1A: the bytes $13, $0F x 6, $1A, $1A. Read by DrawBuilding into DrawSprite's height operand. Not the heights the ship lands on: those are BuildingTops (the world module).
$CE5F ; type 0 the pad $13, factory signs 1-6 $0F (on the roof blocks), flags 7-8 $1A
$CE68 c DrawMover ; Draw robot or shot IY found at cell HL (view row D, column E), after the blocks that overlap it
> Robots and shots keep their real position in +$02/+$03 (x) and +$04 (y) and in +$00/+$01 the cell the walk draws them at. At the real cell FindLatestBlock looks for a later neighbouring block; if there is one, the object is moved there (bit 6 set on it, +$00/+$01 = it) and nothing is drawn yet. When the walk reaches the deferred cell, +$00/+$01 is set back to the real cell, the deferred cell's bit 6 is cleared and the view position is walked back from the deferred cell to the real one (x first, then y), and DrawMoverSprite draws the object there. So a robot is drawn after (over) every block whose sprite overlaps it. 18 were deferred (each drawn later at the walked-back position), 1449 drawn at once. No shot reached here: see DrawCellObject.
