$D33F ; - 32
$D341 ; (the low byte)
$D342 ; borrow: the third above, where D (-1: its line 7) already is
$D343 ; the same third...
$D344 ; ... D up by 8: line 7 of the row above
$D346 ; (D)
$D347 ; DE = one line up
$D348 c TableWord ; HL = the word at HL + 2*A
> The sprite table lookup of DrawSprite, also ConstructionMenu (PartIconLoop $C881) and DrawShape ($BD67). A is an index 0-127 (A*2 must fit a byte). Changes A.
$D348 ; two bytes an entry
$D349 ; HL = the entry
$D34C ; the low byte
$D34D ; the high byte's place
$D34E ; H = the high byte
$D34F ; HL = the word
$D350 ; done
$D351 c AddAToHL ; HL += A
> A changed to the new H; BC, DE kept. Used everywhere a table is indexed by a byte (about 50 call sites).
$D351 ; the low byte
$D352 ; (L)
$D353 ; the carry...
$D354 ; ... into H
$D356 ; (H)
$D357 ; done
$D358 c Random ; A = a pseudo-random byte
> A 32-bit shift register at RandomSeed $FD00-$FD03 (big end first, seeded $39,$30,$39,$30 by Start): shifted left once, the new bit = bit 3 XOR bit 6 of $FD00. Then the byte at the index the previous call used (kept in the operand $D371) gives, by its low 2 bits, the new index, and A = the byte there. HL is kept; A and the flags change. The image holds 3 in that operand (the value it had when the block was saved) and Start does not reset it, so the first call after the start reads $FD03.
$D358 ; HL kept
$D359 ; the four bytes, the top first
$D35C ; the top byte
$D362 ; (two rotations: bit 6 of the sum reaches the carry)
$D365 ; $FD03 gets the feedback bit
$D367 ; (the next byte up)
$D368 ; $FD02
$D36A ; (the next byte up)
$D36B ; $FD01
$D36D ; (the next byte up)
$D36E ; $FD00, the top; its bit 7 drops out
$D372 ; that byte, after the shift
$D375 ; L = the index
$D376 ; kept for the next call
$D37A ; HL back
$D37B ; A = a random byte
$D35D ; bits 6 and 3
$D35F ; bit 6 of the sum = bit 6 XOR bit 3
$D361 ; carry = the feedback bit
$D363 ; shift $FD03..$FD00 left
$D370 ; the index from the last call: the operand $D371
$D373 ; the next index
$D379 ; A = the byte at that index
$D381 PauseLoop ; key 1 held: wait for another control
$D38D ; C = 1: the end marker, shifted out after 8 keys
$D38F KeyLoop ; A = the port's high byte: it selects the key's half row
$D393 ; Z: pressed
$D398 ; pressed: carry 1
$D399 KeyShift ; the first key of the table ends in bit 7
$D39D ; the joystick chosen in the menu
$D3A8 ; keyboard only
$D3AB Kempston ; port $1F: fire, up, down, left, right in bits 4-0
$D3B2 Interface2 ; keys 6-0 (port $EFFE) into the Kempston order
$D3BB ; 0 (fire) to bit 4, 9 (up) to bit 3, 8 (down) to bit 2
$D3C6 ; 7 (right) to bit 0, 6 (left) to bit 1
$D3C7 InputDone ; plus the keys
@module print ; text: the inline print routine, the font characters, big characters, the numbers
@about All text goes through PrintInline: it prints the bytes after its CALL and returns past them, so every string sits inline in the routine that prints it. The bytes: 0 the end, 1 Y X a position (row, column), 2 A the attribute, 3 the next row in the same column, 4 N the size (high nibble the height in rows, low nibble the width in bytes; 0 normal), anything else a character.
@about The chain: PrintInline (in the alternate registers) -> PrintControl -> SetPosition / SetAttr / PrintNewline / SetSize, or a character: PrintChar (8 lines of the 64-glyph font at $6780, codes $20-$5F, glyph = $6680 + 8 * code) or PrintBigChar (each glyph line repeated PrintHeight times, and each pixel doubled for width 2). SetPrintPos turns PrintRow / PrintColumn into PrintAddr and PrintAttrAddr. docs/08 8.2.
@about The numbers: PrintDigit counts how often a negative power of ten fits into HL; E = $20 means "still in the leading zeros", which print as spaces. PrintNumber5 (the day), PrintNumber3, PrintTensUnits, PrintNumber2 (blank leading zero) and PrintNumber2Zero (the clock, the robot counts) are entries into the same chain; PrintCharKeep prints with BC, DE, HL kept. docs/08 8.6.
@about The control handlers read their operand two stack levels up: PrintControl's return address on top, PrintInline's string pointer under it (POP DE; POP HL;... PUSH HL; PUSH DE). Variables: PrintAddr $FD04, PrintAttr $FD13, PrintAttrAddr $FD14, PrintHeight $FD16, PrintWidth $FD17, PrintColumn $FD31, PrintRow $FD32.
$D3E5 c PrintNumber2 ; Print A (0-99) as two digits, a leading zero blank
> In: A. HL = A, E = $20, then PrintTensUnits: a space for a leading zero. Used for the resources (PrintResourceValues), the construction menu values and the miles of ADVANCE / RETREAT. A value of 100 or more would print a tens "digit" above 9 (":" and on; the game caps every resource at 99.
$D3E5 ; HL = the value
$D3E6 ; (H = 0)
$D3E8 ; a leading zero prints as a space
$D3EA ; the tens and the units
$D3EC c PrintNumber2Zero ; Print A (0-99) as two digits with a leading zero
> The clock ($AD90, 6828 calls: "20.20") and the robot counts of the panel.
$D3EC ; HL = the value
$D3ED ; (H = 0)
$D3EF ; every zero prints as 0
$D3F1 ; the tens and the units
$D3F3 c PrintNumber5 ; Print HL as five digits, leading zeros blank
> Used by $AD90 for the day number ("DAY: 4"). Falls into PrintNumber3.
$D3F6 ; leading zeros blank
$D3F8 ; the ten thousands
$D3FE ; the thousands
$D3F3 ; -10000
$D3FB ; -1000
$D401 c PrintNumber3 ; Print HL as three digits (E = $20: leading zeros blank, else printed)
