$D407 c PrintTensUnits ; Print the tens and the units of HL
> HL = the value (0-99, else the tens "digit" runs past 9: 200 prints as "D0"), E = the leading-zero flag. The tens by PrintDigit, the units always printed. The tail of PrintNumber2, PrintNumber2Zero, PrintNumber3 and PrintNumber5.
$D40A ; the tens
$D40E ; as a digit
$D410 ; print it and return
$D407 ; -10
$D40D ; the units digit, always printed
$D413 c PrintDigit ; Print the digit of HL for the power -BC; HL keeps the rest
> Counts how often BC (a negative power of 10) can be added. A zero while E = $20 prints a space and keeps E; any printed digit increments E, so later zeros print as 0.
$D413 ; the count
$D415 ; count it
$D416 ; carry: no borrow, it fitted
$D41A ; A = the digit
$D41B ; not 0: print it
$D41E ; still in the leading zeros?
$D423 ; a zero after a printed digit
$D425 ; as a character
$D414 DigitCountLoop ; take the power once more
$D418 ; one too far: undo
$D41D ; a leading zero
$D420 ; blank: E = $20 is the space
$D424 DigitPrint ; a digit: no more leading zeros
$D427 c PrintCharKeep ; PrintChar A with BC, DE, HL kept (EXX around)
> PrintChar in the alternate registers: the caller's BC, DE, HL are kept (the number printers keep HL, E and the loop counters in them). A and the flags change.
$D427 ; the alternate set
$D428 ; the character
$D42B ; the caller's registers back
$D42C ; done
$D42E PrintInlineLoop ; the next byte after the CALL: the return address moves with it
$D435 ; a control code or a character
