@module difficulty ; the room of the disk and paging pieces, the daily upgrade of the enemy's robots, the enemy's resources
@about $A56A-$A5FF, free space after the sprites in Nether Earth, holds two kinds of bytes. $A56A-$A5D5 is room stage 3 fills with pieces of the patches block (src/patches99): on 128 K paging128 at $A56A (the copy of a text from page 3 and the paging routine $A58A) and diskread at $A598 (the TR-DOS sector reader LOAD MAP calls); on the tape path textload48 and taperead. The block's own bytes there are zeros, but for the four bytes at $A598, the first instruction of diskread.
@about $A5D6-$A5FF holds the two routines of the start-up menu's difficulty levels. The start-up menu (its code at `$DA8E`) writes their calls into the game: MEDIUM and up CALL FillEnemyResources at $B87E (the enemy's build: its resources are 99 again before every robot) and NOP + "??" at $D2C8 (the enemy's robot count hidden); HARD and up 6 NOPs + CALL UpgradeEnemyRobots at $AE41 (the enemy's daily production replaced by the upgrade of its robots); CRITICAL also writes the upgrade's operands, $A5E7 = $FC and $A5E5 = 0. EASY writes back the block's own bytes (the code of Nether Earth). The start-up menu's UPGRADING items toggle bits 7-2 of $A5E7 and set $A5E5 to $F8 when anti-grav (bit 2) is on, else $FF.
@about The parts byte of a robot (+$07): bit 0 bipod, 1 tracks, 2 anti-grav, 3 cannon, 4 missiles, 5 phasers, 6 nuclear, 7 electronics. The image's values, AND $78, OR $84, keep each robot's weapons and give it electronics and anti-grav in place of its chassis.
$A56A s PieceRoomA56A ; Zero: stage 3 copies paging128 or textload48 here ($A56A-$A597)
> On 128 K the patches block's paging128 (41 bytes, `$DA3F`), on one page or tape textload48 (46 bytes, `$DAE7`). The block's 46 zero bytes never run.
$A598 b DiskReadLeftover ; The first 4 bytes of diskread: LD IX,$2F65 (DD 21 65 2F)
> Stage 3 copies diskread (or taperead) over $A598-$A5D5, so these bytes are always replaced. They equal the first instruction of diskread, which reads a sector through the TR-DOS ROM routine $2F65: a leftover of the author's own image, where the reader had been placed.
$A59C s PieceRoomA59C ; Zero: the rest of the reader's room, up to $A5D5
> 58 zero bytes, replaced by diskread's (or taperead's) next bytes.
$A5D6 c UpgradeEnemyRobots ; Every enemy robot's parts: (parts AND [$A5E5]) OR [$A5E7]; HARD and up, once a game day
> Called from DailyProduction ($AE41) when the start-up menu has written the call there (HARD, CRITICAL). For the 24 enemy slots ($DB80, 16 bytes each, alive or not): +$07 = (+$07 AND mask) OR parts. The image's mask $78 keeps the four weapons (cannon, missiles, phasers, nuclear) and drops electronics and the chassis; the parts $84 add electronics and anti-grav. CRITICAL makes the mask 0 and the parts $FC: every enemy robot becomes anti-grav with all four weapons and electronics. UPGRADING in the start-up menu sets the parts' bits 7-2 one by one and the mask to $F8 (anti-grav chosen: the old chassis bits 0-1 dropped) or $FF (all kept).
> Only +$07 changes: the height +$09 its build computed from the parts stays as it was. The view draws a robot from its part bits (DrawRobot), so the new parts show at once, and the weapons code reads the same bits. A free slot gets parts too, but a new robot's record is written whole by its build. B = 24; IY kept.
$A5D6 ; IY kept for the caller
$A5D8 ; the record size
$A5DB ; the enemy's 24 slots
$A5DD ; IY = the first enemy record
$A5E1 UpgradeEnemyRobot ; the robot's parts
$A5E4 ; AND the mask: $78 keeps the weapons (CRITICAL 0)
$A5E6 ; OR the new parts: $84 electronics + anti-grav (CRITICAL $FC)
$A5E8 ; back into the record
$A5EB ; the next slot
$A5ED ; all 24
$A5EF ; IY back
$A5F2 c FillEnemyResources ; The enemy's resources = 99 each; HL = $FD4A
> Writes $63 (99) into the 8 bytes $FD4A-$FD51: the enemy's seven resources (EnemyResources: general, electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis) and Side, which the side's searches read only as zero or not (the enemy's 1 becomes 99, same meaning). Returns HL = $FD4A, so that a CALL to it can replace LD HL,$FD4A: called by NewGameSetup ($A688) for every level and, MEDIUM and up, by the enemy's build ($B87E), so the enemy never runs short.
$A5F2 ; the enemy's resources
$A5F5 ; 8 bytes (Side too)
$A5F7 FillEnemyResource ; 99
$A5F9 ; the next
$A5FA ; 8 times
$A5FC ; HL = $FD4A, as the LD HL it replaces

@module tables ; Lookup tables of the view and the robots: view edges, part sprites, sprite pointers, heights
@about Small constant tables the view and the movement code index by type. Resource: ViewEdgeA / ViewEdgeB are src/res/game99/gfx/view-edges.png (tools/game99_res.py); the others stay in the source as rows the code reads field by field.
@about Readers: DrawViewBackground / DrawEdgeStrips (the edges), DirectionLoop and ConstructionMenu (PartSpriteTable), DrawSprite through TableWord (SpriteTable, index id x 2 + parity), DirectionLoop and SpawnHeightLoop (PartHeights), TerrainTop (TerrainHeights). None is written.
$D6A8 b ViewEdgeA 2 ; View edge, black on the left: 8 lines of 2 bytes
@res gfx/view-edges.bin
> $FFFF, $FFFC, $FFF0... $C000: a staircase 2 pixels a line. DrawViewBackground copies it with DrawEdgeStrips (2 bytes a line, 8 lines, then 2 bytes left) 6 times from $5B0A, the lower edge of the black triangle above the map, and 3 times from $5FB2. Image: d1-img/D6A8-ViewEdges.png.
$D6B8 b ViewEdgeB 2 ; View edge, black on the right: 8 lines of 2 bytes
@res gfx/view-edges.bin
> $0003, $000F... $FFFF; DrawViewBackground copies it 4 times from $6512: with the 3 copies of ViewEdgeA at $5FB2 it makes the black band along the map's front edge, bottom right of the view.
$D6C8 b PartSpriteTable 4 ; Robot part sprites: 8 parts x 4 directions, sprite id - $16
> Row = part (bipod, tracks, anti-grav, cannon, missiles, phasers, nuclear, electronics), column = direction (the number of the lowest set bit of robot +$08: 0 X+, 1 X-, 2 Y+, 3 Y-). Read by DirectionLoop ($CF16; sprite id = byte + $16, then DrawSprite) for robots in the view and by ConstructionMenu ($C87B; column 1, the odd-column sprite via SpriteTable+$58) for the part icons. Bipod, tracks and missiles have one picture per axis, anti-grav and nuclear one for all four.
> The rows, as sprite ids: bipod $18, $18, $19, $19; tracks $16, $16, $17, $17; anti-grav $1A x 4; cannon $1B-$1E; missiles $1F, $1F, $20, $20; phasers $21-$24; nuclear $25 x 4; electronics $26-$29. Note the part order of the rows (bipod first) against the sprite order (tracks first).
$D6C8 ; part 0 bipod (+$07 bit 0): ids 24 24 25 25 (along X, along Y; columns: facing X+, X-, Y+, Y-
$D6CC ; part 1 tracks (+$07 bit 1): ids 22 22 23 23 (along X, along Y)
$D6D0 ; part 2 anti-grav (+$07 bit 2): id 26 for all four
$D6D4 ; part 3 cannon (+$07 bit 3): ids 27-30
$D6D8 ; part 4 missiles (+$07 bit 4): ids 31 31 32 32 (X, Y)
$D6DC ; part 5 phasers (+$07 bit 5): ids 33-36
$D6E0 ; part 6 nuclear (+$07 bit 6): id 37 for all four
$D6E4 ; part 7 electronics (+$07 bit 7): ids 38-41
$D6E8 w SpriteTable 2 ; Sprite pointers: 51 sprite ids x (even column, odd column)
> Word = the sprite block for index id x 2 + parity (DrawSprite: AND $3F, SRA L / ADC A,A, then TableWord). Ids 0 ship, 1 shadow, 2-18 and 21 map types (2-5 stones, 6-7 boulders, 8-11 rocks, 12-14 trench, 15-18 blocks, 21 post), 19-20 unused (they point at the ship), 22-41 robot parts (PartSpriteTable), 42 the player's flag, 43 the enemy's flag, 44 the warbase sign, 45-50 shots (cannon, missile, phaser; X then Y). ConstructionMenu reads from SpriteTable+$58 (id 22 on). Read by TableWord ($D34C/$D34E).
$D6E8 ; id 0: the player's ship; each row: the even and the odd column's block
$D6EC ; id 1: the ship's shadow
$D6F0 ; id 2: map type 2: stones A
$D6F4 ; id 3: map type 3: stones B
$D6F8 ; id 4: map type 4: stones C
$D6FC ; id 5: map type 5: stones D
$D700 ; id 6: map type 6: boulders A
$D704 ; id 7: map type 7: boulders B
$D708 ; id 8: map type 8: rocks A
$D70C ; id 9: map type 9: rocks B
$D710 ; id 10: map type 10: rocks C
$D714 ; id 11: map type 11: rocks D
$D718 ; id 12: map type 12: trench start (along Y, along X)
$D71C ; id 13: map type 13: trench middle (along Y, along X)
$D720 ; id 14: map type 14: trench end (along Y, along X)
$D724 ; id 15: map type 15: low block
$D728 ; id 16: map type 16: tall block with an arch
$D72C ; id 17: map type 17: low block (door, window)
$D730 ; id 18: map type 18: tall block (grid, dotted)
$D734 ; id 19: unused: points at the ship
$D738 ; id 20: unused: points at the ship
$D73C ; id 21: map type 21: the border post
$D740 ; id 22: tracks along X (part 1, X+ / X-; ConstructionMenu reads from here (SpriteTable+$58)
$D744 ; id 23: tracks along Y (part 1, Y+ / Y-; also the chassis factory's sign
$D748 ; id 24: bipod along X (part 0)
$D74C ; id 25: bipod along Y (part 0)
$D750 ; id 26: anti-grav (part 2, every direction)
$D754 ; id 27: cannon facing X+ (part 3)
$D758 ; id 28: cannon facing X-
$D75C ; id 29: cannon facing Y+ (also the cannon factory's sign)
$D760 ; id 30: cannon facing Y-
$D764 ; id 31: missiles along X (part 4)
$D768 ; id 32: missiles along Y (also the missiles factory's sign)
$D76C ; id 33: phasers facing X+ (part 5)
$D770 ; id 34: phasers facing X-
$D774 ; id 35: phasers facing Y+ (also the phasers factory's sign)
$D778 ; id 36: phasers facing Y-
$D77C ; id 37: nuclear (part 6, every direction; also the nuclear factory's sign)
$D780 ; id 38: electronics facing X+ (part 7)
$D784 ; id 39: electronics facing X-
$D788 ; id 40: electronics facing Y+ (also the electronics factory's sign)
$D78C ; id 41: electronics facing Y-
$D790 ; id 42: the player's flag (building type 7)
$D794 ; id 43: the enemy's flag (building type 8)
$D798 ; id 44: the warbase sign (building type 0, the pad)
$D79C ; id 45: shot: cannon shell along X (weapon 1)
$D7A0 ; id 46: shot: cannon shell along Y
$D7A4 ; id 47: shot: missile along X (weapon 2)
$D7A8 ; id 48: shot: missile along Y
$D7AC ; id 49: shot: phaser beam along X (weapon 3)
$D7B0 ; id 50: shot: phaser beam along Y
$D7B4 b PartHeights 8 ; Height of each robot part in units (bipod $0B, tracks 7, anti-grav 8, cannon 6, missiles 6, phasers 7, nuclear 7, electronics 7)
> Indexed by the part number. DirectionLoop ($CF28) adds it to the draw height after each part, so the parts stack; SpawnHeightLoop ($B911) sums it over robot +$07 into +$09, the robot's height, when a robot is made.
$D7B4 ; by part: bipod 11, tracks 7, anti-grav 8, cannon 6, missiles 6, phasers 7, nuclear 7, electronics 7
$D7BC b TerrainHeights 8 ; Height of each map cell type (0-22)
> Indexed by cell AND $1F: 0-1 0, stones 2-5 2, boulders 6-7 3, rocks 8-11 6, trench 12-14 0, blocks 15 7, 16 15, 17 7, 18 15, 19-20 0, the post 21 99 (no flight over it), 22 0. Read only by TerrainTop ($B093), the height under a cell for CheckShipMove (CellTop) and for shots and robots (GroundUnderFootprint).
$D7BC ; types 0-7: 0 empty 0, 1 0, stones 2-5 2, boulders 6-7 3
$D7C4 ; types 8-15: rocks 8-11 6, trench 12-14 0, low block 15 7
$D7CC ; types 16-22: tall block 16 15, low block 17 7, tall block 18 15, 19-20 0, post 21 99 (no flight), 22 0

@equ $5B00 ViewBuffer 3200 ; The view's back buffer: 160 lines of 20 bytes (160 x 160 pixels)
@equ $D7D3 Shots 45 ; Shots in flight: 5 records of 9 bytes
@equ $D800 RadarOwn 256 ; Radar bitmap with the player's robots: 16 lines of 16 bytes
@equ $D900 RadarEnemy 43 ; Radar bitmap with the enemy's robots: 16 lines of 16 bytes
@equ $D92B SaveShots 45 ; Save / load staging: the 5 Shots records
@equ $D958 SaveBuildings 168 ; Save / load staging: the 56 Buildings records
@equ $DA00 Robots 384 ; The player's robots: slots 0-23, 16 bytes a record
@equ $DB80 EnemyRobots 384 ; The enemy's robots: slots 24-47, the same records
@equ $DD00 Map 8154 ; The map: 16 rows of 512 cells, cell = $DD00 + $200 x Y + X
@equ $FCDA MapTail 15 ; The map's last 15 image bytes: they load over the loader N1
@equ $FD00 RandomSeed 4 ; the shift register of Random, seeded $39,$30,$39,$30 by Start
@equ $FD04 PrintAddr 2 ; the screen address of the next printed character
@equ $FD06 MapView 2 ; the map byte at the view's left column: $DD00 + ViewX
@equ $FD08 SavedSP 2 ; SP kept while ClearViewBuffer pushes and while the menu music runs
@equ $FD0A ViewX 2 ; the map column at the view's left edge, follows ShipX
@equ $FD0C Input ; the controls read this game turn: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire, 5 save, 6 abort, 7 pause
@equ $FD0D ShipY ; the ship's row, 1-15
@equ $FD0E ShipX 2 ; the ship's column, $000E-$01F5
@equ $FD10 Altitude ; the ship's height: fire raises it by 2 to $30, released it sinks by 1 to what is under it
@equ $FD11 ShipDrawPos 2 ; the ship's view position while its drawing is deferred (column, row); 0 = not deferred
@equ $FD13 PrintAttr ; the attribute of printed characters
@equ $FD14 PrintAttrAddr 2 ; the attribute address of the next printed character
@equ $FD16 PrintHeight ; big characters: each font row printed this many times (PrintInline code 4, high nibble)
@equ $FD17 PrintWidth ; big characters: the width in bytes, 0 normal (PrintInline code 4, low nibble)
@equ $FD1A RadarPhase ; toggled each frame: 0 shows the player's radar layer, 1 the enemy's
@equ $FD1B RadarLeft ; the radar window's left edge in units of 8 map columns, 0-$30
@equ $FD1C RadarColumn 2 ; RadarLeft x 8: the radar window's first map column
@equ $FD1E RadarBlink ; bit 0: the ship's radar blip is drawn
@equ $FD1F MenuCursor 2 ; the construction menu: the column (0 EXIT MENU, 1 START ROBOT, 2 the parts), the part row; the robot menu: its last item
@equ $FD21 MenuParts ; the parts chosen, bit = part row; the new robot's +$07
@equ $FD22 Resources 7 ; general, electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis
@equ $FD29 MenuResources 7 ; the copy the construction menu spends; back to Resources on START ROBOT
@equ $FD30 ClimbTurns ; game turns the ship still climbs by itself: 3 at a new game, 5 after a menu
@equ $FD31 PrintColumn ; the print position's column
@equ $FD32 PrintRow ; the print position's row
@equ $FD33 TitleColourPhase ; 0-15, TitleColourCycle's frame counter
@equ $FD34 TurnFrames ; frames counted by PlayInterrupt; a game turn waits for 10
@equ $FD35 Clock 4 ; minutes (by 5), hours, days (16 bits)
@equ $FD39 PanelBusy ; 1 while a robot menu owns the right panel
@equ $FD3A PlayerBuildings 7 ; the player's buildings by type: warbases, then factories in the Resources order
@equ $FD41 PlayerRobotCount ; live robots in slots 0-23
@equ $FD42 EnemyBuildings 7 ; the enemy's buildings, the same order; no warbase left = the game is won
@equ $FD49 EnemyRobotCount ; live robots in slots 24-47
@equ $FD4A EnemyResources 7 ; the enemy's resources, the order of Resources
@equ $FD51 Side ; the side being processed: 0 the player's, 1 the enemy's
@equ $FD52 RadarDirty ; 1: rebuild the radar layers from the map at the next ship mark
@equ $FD53 SoundFx ; 1-$7F a falling tone (fire), $FC a miss, $FA a hit, $C8 a robot destroyed; PlayInterrupt counts it on
@equ $FD54 BassReturnPos 2 ; menu music: the bass track's return point
@equ $FD56 LeadReturnPos 2 ; menu music: the lead track's return point
@equ $FD70 Warbases 20 ; 4 capture points of 5 bytes: X (2), Y, owner (bit 6 player, 5 enemy, 7 destroyed) + type, turns a robot has stood at the door (144 captures)
@equ $FD84 Factories 120 ; 24 capture points of 5 bytes, the same shape
@equ $FDFE Handler 2 ; the interrupt handler: the IM 2 vector's JP at $FDFD jumps here
@equ $FF01 Buildings 168 ; 56 records of 3 bytes: the map cell address, the type (0 a warbase pad, 1-6 factories, 7 the player's flag, 8 the enemy's)
